- Fairness and starvation mitigation via deadlines
- Realistic testing with mixed workloads and tail latency focus

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal C-like sketch (conceptual)
typedef struct io_req { uint64_t lba; uint32_t len; int id; struct io_req *next; } io_req;
typedef struct { io_req *head, *tail; } ring_t;
static inline void io_enqueue(ring_t *q, io_req *r) { r->next = NULL; /* CAS-based tail append */ }
static inline io_req* io_dequeue(ring_t *q) { /* pop head */ return NULL; }
static inline int can_merge(io_req *a, io_req *b) { return a->lba + a->len == b->lba; }
static inline io_req* merge_adjacent(io_req *a, io_req *b) {
  if (!can_merge(a,b)) return NULL; a->len += b->len; return a;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests memory-management basics: forking, page sharing, and Copy-On-Write semantics, plus race-free fault handling and TLB coherence.

## Key Concepts
- Copy-On-Write (COW)
- Page tables and PTE flags (RO, Writable, Present)
- Atomic update to mappings and per-page refcounts
- TLB flushing and race avoidance on faults
- Testing with concurrent writes and rapid forking

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-skeleton for a COW fault handler
void handle_cow_fault(Process *p, void *fault_addr) {
  Page *shared = get_page(p, fault_addr);
  if (!shared->cow) return; // unexpected
  Page *private = alloc_page();
  copy_page(private, shared->addr);
  // atomically remap fault_addr to private with W permissions
  update_pte(p, fault_addr, private, WRITE);
  decrement_ref(shared);
  flush_tlb(p, fault_addr);
}
```

