## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of kernel-level blocking I/O, wait queues, wakeups, and the data transfer path from kernel to user space. It's accessible to beginners while revealing familiarity with synchronization hazards and scheduler interactions.

## Key Concepts
- Blocking I/O and wait queues
- Wakeup mechanisms and scheduler interactions
- copy_to_user data transfer path from kernel to user space
- Context switching and task state management
- Pipe buffer management and synchronization

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo kernel path sketch (simplified)
ssize_t pipe_read(struct file *f, void __user *buf, size_t count) {
  struct pipe_inode_info *pipe = f->private_data;

  if (pipe_len(pipe) == 0) {
    if (f->f_flags & O_NONBLOCK)
      return -EAGAIN;

    // Block current task on pipe's wait queue
    wait_event_interruptible(pipe->wait, 
                            pipe_len(pipe) > 0 || pipe->writers == 0);
  }

  if (pipe_len(pipe) == 0 && pipe->writers == 0)
    return 0; // EOF

  // Copy data to user space
  ret = copy_to_user(buf, pipe->buf + pipe->cur, count);
  pipe->cur += count;
  pipe->len -= count;

  // Wake up writers if space available
  if (pipe->len < PIPE_BUF)
    wake_up_interruptible(&pipe->wait);

  return count;
}

ssize_t pipe_write(struct file *f, const void __user *buf, size_t count) {
  struct pipe_inode_info *pipe = f->private_data;

  // Wait for space if pipe is full
  wait_event_interruptible(pipe->wait,
                          pipe_len(pipe) < PIPE_BUF || pipe->readers == 0);

  // Copy from user to kernel pipe buffer
  copy_from_user(pipe->buf + pipe->len, buf, count);
  pipe->len += count;

  // Wake up blocked readers
  wake_up_interruptible(&pipe->wait);

  return count;
}
```

## Practical Relevance
Understanding this flow is crucial for:
- Debugging deadlocks and race conditions in process communication
- Performance optimization of IPC mechanisms
- System call implementation and driver development
- Real-time system design with predictable latency
- Container and microservice communication patterns

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic concurrency primitives, memory ordering, and a practical IPC primitive from first principles.

## Key Concepts
- Bounded ring buffer fundamentals
- Atomic indices and memory ordering
- Data visibility vs. race-free updates
- Simple correctness testing under contention

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo C-like sketch
#define N 1024
int buf[N];
atomic_size_t head = 0, tail = 0;

int enqueue(int v){
  size_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  size_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&head, memory_order_acquire);
  size_t next = (t + 1) % N;
  if (next == h) return -1; // full
  buf[t] = v;
  atomic_store_explicit(&tail, next, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}

int dequeue(int *out){
  size_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&head, memory_order_relaxed);
  size_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (h == t) return -1; // empty
  *out = buf[h];
  atomic_store_explicit(&head, (h + 1) % N, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers?
- How would you detect and recover from stack/memory ordering mistakes?

